hey guys Greg knuckles here with string  theory calm back in the second  installment of our daily Q&amp;A series so  two days in still still every day so  that's a that's a good start I would say  question today is do I need to train  different muscles differently should I  train them in different rep ranges based  on fiber type breakdown I thought this  was a good question because I see this  get asked a lot I think this is a common  idea out there that particular muscles  are either predominantly type 1 muscle  fibers type 2 muscle fibers and because  of that you should train them  differently I am not really on board  with this idea for three main reasons  the first is that kind of in general  just as a starting point most muscles  don't really have one predominant fiber  type some notable exceptions there your  soleus one of the muscles in your calves  they are typically 85% Plus slow-twitch  type 1 fibers and some of your eye  muscles and also forearm muscles finger  extensors some of those are 80 90 % plus  type 2 muscle fibers but the majority of  the muscles in your body are essentially  pretty close to a 50/50 mix of type 1  and type 2 muscle fibers now there is  some inter individual variation there  some people are very very fast twitch  dominant some people are very very slow  twitch dominant in general but when kind  of dealing with averages so if you're  giving typical advice like most people  should train hamstrings with low reps  because they're predominantly fast which  that's something I hear a lot there's  really not good evidence to support that  hamstrings pecs Bell  slats there's quads there are multiple  studies looking at fiber types in those  muscles and a page with a breakdown of  all of those studies will be linked in  the description of this video and what  you'll see is that you know some studies  maybe 60/40 65 35 something like that  but the majority of studies for the  majority of muscles do kind of cluster  around a pretty even mix of type 1 and  type 2 muscle fibers so that's that's my  first problem with this idea one most  muscles aren't really predominantly type  1 or type 2 in general now the second  thing is people have this idea that you  can test your muscles to see if they are  predominantly type 1 or type 2 by taking  a particular percentage of your 1 rep  max and taking it to failure  so maybe 80% for as many reps as  possible if you get fewer than 8 reps  it's probably mostly fast-twitch fibers  because it gave out faster if you can  get more than 8 reps and it's probably  predominantly slow-twitch fibers that's  that's a test I see some people  recommending to determine what your  predominant fiber type in a particular  muscle is but there's a problem with  this as well this has been studied twice  I would need to look for these papers in  one of them though found no significant  correlation whatsoever between  predominant muscle fiber type and how  many reps people could get with a given  percentage of their one rep max the  other one did find a significant  correlation but if memory serves the  correlation coefficient was like point  4.5 which means the r-squared the  coefficient of determination was like  point 1 6 2 point 2 5 something like  that so in other words less than a  quarter of the variation in number of  reps people could get was explained by  fiber type itself  so they're they're very likely is some  relationship between the predominant  fiber type in a muscle and how many reps  you could get with a given percentage of  your one rep max if if you wanted to try  to test it like that but it's not a very  sensitive or accurate test so you know  odds odds would be pretty good that  unless you were just on the very extreme  end of the spectrum so you know maybe if  you were comparing your your finger  extensors to your soleus very very  predominantly fast twitch versus very  very predominantly slow twitch muscle  you know that very well may be a  meaningful difference but for pretty  much every other muscle in your body  that test would pick up a lot more noise  than signal so there's there's not  really a good practical way to know if  if one of your muscles are multiple if  your muscles are predominantly one fiber  type or not the only way to know for  sure is actually to get muscle biopsies  and if you want to go to a lab and have  people stick big needles and all of your  muscles and rip out little chunks of  your flesh all over your body then you  can know what's your predominant fiber  type is in each muscle but most most  people aren't going to do that so first  problem just in general most muscles are  a pretty even mix of fiber types - if  you're if you're concerned for yourself  that you know maybe some of your muscles  are predominantly type 1 or type 2 which  can certainly happen there's no easy  test for it simply seeing how many reps  you can get with a given percentage of  your 1 rep max  that may that may be that is probably  correlated with fiber type to some  degree but it's just not a very good  test it's not a very sensitive test  picks up a lot more noise than signal  and the last thing is even if you did  know that one particular muscle was say  much more type one dominance  whatever like let's say you go to a lab  you get your quads biopsied and normal  person's quads would be about 50/50 type  1 and type 2 let's say yours are 80%  type 2 does that mean that your best bet  would be to train your quads heavy  explosively you know titans types of  things that you would think would would  be better for type 2 fiber growth and  here's here's the real issue there's  really not good evidence that that would  make a meaningful difference studies are  all over the place here for the most  part what you tend to see is regardless  of rep range used type 1 or type 2  fibers tend to grow between 50 and 75  percent more than type 1 muscle fibers  do we really with almost any rep range  there are some studies that that could  be used to argue preferential type 1  versus type 2 fiber growth but there  they come out on on both sides of the  issue so one buy I know I'm going to  butcher this researchers name but  Schwing a I believe actually found that  high rep training that people would tend  to think would be better for type 1  fiber growth calls growth of type ii  fibers but not type 1 fibers whereas  lower rep training caused growth of both  types of fibers so that that would lead  you to believe that if you want type 1  fibers to grow those slow twitch fibers  you would need heavy training for that  and that lighter training is ineffective  for that which would run kind of counter  to this theory the but then there was  another study by mitchell that was  comparing working with 80% versus 30%  and found that overall muscle growth was  the same but that there was a lot more  type 1 muscle fiber growth slow twitch  muscle fiber growth with the lighter  higher rep training which would kind of  be in line with this theory so evidence  evidence is mixed and it's really really  inconclusive weather lighter higher rep  training is actually more beneficial for  type one slow-twitch muscle fiber growth  and whether heavier more explosive  training would be better for type 2 fast  twitch muscle fiber growth so just kind  of to recap I don't think when so okay  assuming that your primary goal is just  hypertrophy to get as jacked as possible  your best bet across the board is  probably just to train with a variety of  rep ranges regardless of what muscle  you're talking about the idea that you  should train different muscles  differently based on fiber type  breakdown  probably doesn't hold much water that  doesn't necessarily mean that you do  have to train every muscle exactly the  same that doesn't mean that there aren't  other factors that may influence how you  train particular muscles but to base a  lot of how you're training a muscle on  what its predominant fiber type is in my  opinion that doesn't hold very much  water because one most muscles that  you'd be dealing with except maybe your  soleus are probably going to be a pretty  even mix of type 1 and type 2 muscle  fibers in the first place - there's not  a good easy test - that has much  predictive power - to get a good idea of  whether a particular muscle is type 1 or  type 2 fiber dominant and the last thing  is even if you could know that the  evidence at this point is very very  mixed very unclear about whether high or  low rep training can specifically target  a particular fiber type so in short I  think I think it's an interesting idea  and I think it's a ripe area for  research that you know may either kind  of rain on this parade a little bit more  or validate it a bit more in the future  we'll just see as things move forward  but at this point when planning a  training program I don't really think  again for  hypertrophy specifically so this is not  about endurance training this is not  about power training this is just simply  about trying to get jacked because this  is where this idea is typically applied  for hypertrophy training I don't really  think this is this is something you  should really be using as a main guiding  principle for program design for most  people most of the time your best bet is  just going to be to use a variety of rep  ranges regardless all right guys so that  is the video if you liked it please like  and subscribe if you have training  questions that you want answered ask  them below and I will pick my favorite  one and answer it tomorrow and until  then enjoy your training and I will see  you in that next video
 

